  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Platforms (North) keeps compounding on adviser activations and managed-portfolio flows (~$3bn/half net inflows, AUM +10-11%/yr) with ~2bps/yr fee-margin compression (FY26 guided 40-41bps vs 42bps FY25), so NPAT grows ~10% fading to 3%. S&I has just reached cashflow break-even; growth is market beta net of fee caps. NZ Wealth tracks KiwiSaver contributions. AMP Bank absorbs GO start-up losses through 2027 then recovers to ~$66-70m by Y3-4 (NIM guided flat ~1.25%, GO deposits scaling toward the $2bn target, ROC back to 5-6%). Partnerships & Group steps down in Y1 (surplus-capital interest stripped, buyback cash gone, China partnership growth slows from the 107% 1H26 spike to ~10%) then grows 3-4%. Below-the-line items persist at 10% of underlying NPAT for two years then settle at 5% - AMP has never had a clean statutory year outside disposal gains. 10% of statutory profit retained for bank capital. CoE 10.5%, g 2.5%, 15x cap (Gordon at 12.8x binds).",
    "bull": "North wins platform share faster (AUM +13%, margin compression slower as managed portfolios scale), S&I turns to sustained net inflows, AMP Bank GO reaches breakeven by Y2 and the bank's ROC rises toward 7%, China's CLPC keeps compounding at 10%+ as the pension market opens, cost-out delivers a cost-to-income ratio under 55%, and remediation finally fades to 3% of NPAT. g 3.0%, 15x cap.",
    "bear": "An equity-market drawdown plus fee caps stall Platforms at ~3% and put S&I back into net outflows; NIM pressure and a failed GO scale-up hold the bank at $43-50m; China partnership earnings halve on regulatory or repatriation constraints (they were 32% of 1H26 underlying NPAT); a new remediation/litigation cycle keeps below-the-line items at 15-20% of NPAT; mega super funds use DBFO Tranche 2 to take retirement flows from adviser platforms; new CEO Blair Vernon's strategy reset consumes capital. g 1.5%, 12x cap."
  },
